Chain traced: table dbo.PrivateMessage -> stored procedures that read/write it (spSelectUserPrivateMessage, spSelectUserNewPrivateMessage, spSelectReadPrivateMessage, spSelectReadSentPrivateMessage, spSelectPrivateMessageStatistic, spSelectPrivateMessages, spSelectPrivateMessageByPMID, spSelectCountPMinTrashByUserID, spUpdateMarkPMUnreadOrRead, spUpdateMarkedPMOldMsgViaAJAX, spMovePMToTrash, spMovePMBackToInbox, spInsertPrivateMessage, spDeletePrivateMessage, spDeleteAllPMInTrash, spSelectPMReadingMsgValidation, spSelectPMReadingSentMsgValidation, and spSelectSiteStatistics's TotalPrivateMessage column) -> C# wrapper methods in ActionStoredPrecedure.cs (Blogic) -> BLL provider classes (ProviderPMStatistic, ProviderPrivateMessageViewInbox, ProviderReadMessage, ProviderReadSentMessage, PrivateMessageRepository, ProviderSiteStatistics) -> .aspx.cs code-behind and .ascx.cs user controls (Control/Login.ascx.cs calls Blogic.GetUserNewPrivateMessageCount on every authenticated page load; Control/pmmenu.ascx.cs calls GetUserNewPrivateMessageCount/GetPMinTrashByUserID/ProviderPMStatistic) -> .aspx pages.

Key finding: Control/Login.ascx is registered inside SiteTemplate.master and SiteTemplate2.master (confirmed via grep), so the 36 .aspx pages that set MasterPageFile to either master inherit a live dependency on the PrivateMessage table through Login.ascx's ShowPrivateMessageAlert() method. Three more pages (pmsend.aspx, editarticle.aspx, addarticle.aspx) register Control/Login.ascx directly without a master page. AjaxRequest.aspx's code-behind directly calls MarkPMasOldMsgViaAjax, MarkMessageUnreadOrRead, MovePMToTrash, MovePMBackToInbox, PrivateMessageRepository.Delete, and DeleteAllPMInTrash across its mode-dispatch switch. admin/managermain.aspx calls ProviderSiteStatistics.fillup(), which reads TotalPrivateMessage from spSelectSiteStatistics (a direct COUNT(*) against dbo.PrivateMessage). Verified exclusions: pmblockedusers.aspx.cs, myaccount.aspx.cs (beyond its ProviderSiteStatistics call already counted), and userprofile.aspx.cs only touch PrivateMessageBlockedUsers/other tables directly in their own code; unittestpage.aspx.cs has its only PrivateMessage-related call commented out; confirmupdateprivatemsg.aspx, confirmpmsent.aspx, and confirmupdatepmconfig.aspx contain no PrivateMessage references and don't use either master page."}
